Licence history
---------------

Every commit on this repository's main branch is AGPL-3.0-or-later.  There is no
MIT-licensed commit on that branch, so no "MIT release remains valid for earlier
commits" carve-out applies to it.

There was an earlier MIT release, and it was a release of *this same repository*.
This repository was created in the author's personal namespace as
`ericyoshida/airsim-uav-3d-detection` and was later transferred into the
FLYMOV-ERC organisation; the transfer moved the repository, it did not fork or
re-publish it, and GitHub's permanent redirect from the former path is in place.
Under that former name, the working tree was published under the MIT licence on
6 June 2026 as a single flat commit (2fddc2c, 243 files, `LICENSE` = MIT,
Copyright (c) 2026 Eric Ezequiel Yoshida de Lima).  That commit is preserved here
under the tag

    archive/flat-dump-2026-06

The branch `main` was afterwards rewritten as a fresh root commit, so the tagged
commit is not an ancestor of `main`: one repository, two disjoint histories -- the
tag holding the MIT-licensed flat dump, `main` holding the AGPL-3.0 reorganisation
of it.  Anyone who obtained a copy under the MIT terms keeps their MIT rights to
that copy, and nothing here revokes them.

Disclosure about the archived commit: the tree at `archive/flat-dump-2026-06` is
the unedited June 2026 dump and still carries absolute developer-machine paths
(43 lines across 27 files hard-code the author's home working directories, in
POSIX form and, in one file, in the Windows/WSL form of the same directory).  It
is kept, unmodified and unredacted, solely so that the artifact the MIT grant
attaches to remains verifiable; nothing on `main` reads or reproduces those
paths.

Why this tree is AGPL-3.0: it ships code that imports Ultralytics YOLO, which is
itself licensed AGPL-3.0.  The two-dimensional detection stage is built on it.
Twelve modules import `ultralytics` directly, and seven more reach it
transitively through `common/pipeline.py`.  Distributing a combined work that
links an AGPL-3.0 library under MIT is not permitted by that licence, so this
tree was brought into line with its strongest dependency rather than the other
way round.  The alternative -- isolating every Ultralytics import behind an optional
extra so that an MIT core never links it -- was considered and rejected as
misleading, since the pipeline the dissertation describes does not function
without the YOLO stage.


